-- title:
--   Tame inertia on the Drinfeld chart: semilinear, linear part ctcdotdiag(1,d^{-q})
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a natural number $M'\neq 0$ with $q\nmid M'$ (hypothesis `hqM'`), and a prime $\ell$ with $\ell\equiv 11\pmod{12}$ and $\ell\mid M'$.
--
--   *Constant field.* $L$ is a field of characteristic zero, $\zeta\in L$ a primitive $q$-th root of unity, $\xi\in L$ a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity, with $\zeta=\xi^{\ell}$, and `hι` requires the existence of a ring homomorphism $\iota:L\to\mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\xi)=\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$.
--
--   *Level and function field.* $H_1$ is a subgroup of $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$ equal to the intersection of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22), the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb{Z}/\ell)^\times$. The intermediate field $K$ of $L\subseteq\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ is $L$-generated by the image, under the coefficientwise map $\mathbb{Q}\to L$, of the $q$-expansion function field [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L79) of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$.
--
--   *Base ring.* $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with $L$ as its fraction field and algebraically closed residue field, such that $q$ lies in the maximal ideal $\mathfrak m_A$ (`hAq`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ (`hζA`); $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $A\to L\to K$. The element $j\in K$ has Laurent series the image of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant, and $j\neq 0$. The element $\varpi\in A$ generates $\mathfrak m_A$, and $t\in A$ satisfies $t^{q-1}=q\,w$ for some unit $w$ (`ht`).
--
--   *Point of the integral model.* $\mathfrak X=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) is the pushout of the two spectra of the chart algebras attached to $j$ and $j^{-1}$; $z$ is a point of $\mathfrak X$, and $\varpi z$ is, by `hϖz`, the germ at $z$ of the image of $\varpi$ under the structure morphism $\mathfrak X\to\operatorname{Spec}A$, assumed (`hz`) to lie in the maximal ideal of the stalk $\mathcal O_{\mathfrak X,z}$. The point $y$ of $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the finite chart algebra $C=$ `chartAlgFin A ↥K j` maps to $z$ under `ιFin` (`hy`), and `hss` requires that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi:C\to\Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), i.e. every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero point killed by $q$.
--
--   *Drinfeld chart.* $W$ is a discrete valuation domain, complete for the $\mathfrak m_W$-adic topology, $\sigma:A\to W$ a ring homomorphism with $\mathfrak m_W=(\sigma\varpi)$; $f,u,v\in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u,v$ units and $f\equiv X_0X_1^{q}-X_0^{q}X_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (`hf`, the Drinfeld form [`DrinfeldCurve.LocalChart.drinfeldForm`](def/DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart.html#L18)); and $e$ is a ring isomorphism from the $\mathfrak m$-adic completion of $\mathcal O_{\mathfrak X,z}$ onto $S=W[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma t)\,v-f\,u)$. The abbreviations introduced are: `STK` $=\mathcal O_{\mathfrak X,z}$, `CMP` its completion, `toC` the completion map, $S$ as above with quotient map `mkS`, and `germY` the canonical ring homomorphism $C\to\mathcal O_{\mathfrak X,z}$ obtained from the identification of $C$ with the sections of `ιFin` over the top open and the germ map at $z=\iota_{\mathrm{Fin}}(y)$.
--
--   The assertion is an implication with two antecedents about $e$.
--
--   First antecedent (constants): for every $a\in A$, $e$ carries the image in the completion of the germ at $z$ of $a$ (via $\mathfrak X\to\operatorname{Spec}A$) to the class of the constant series $C(\sigma a)$.
--
--   Second antecedent (level equivariance with linear part, summarised conjunct by conjunct): for every $\gamma\in\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ lying in $\Gamma_0(M')$ and every $L$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ satisfying [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) — that is, for all weights $k$, all modular forms $f,g$ of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g$, $p_g$ giving a nonzero Laurent series, all $x\in K$ whose Laurent series is the image of $p_f/p_g$, and all $\iota:L\to\mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\zeta)=\exp(2\pi i/q)$, one has $\iota(\tau x)\cdot(g\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1})^{\wedge}=(f\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1})^{\wedge}$ on $q$-expansions, where `conjElemN q γ⁻¹` is $\gamma^{-1}$ conjugated so that its upper-right entry is divided and its lower-left entry multiplied by $q$ — and for every proof that $\tau$ preserves $C$, if the induced endomorphism of $C$ is congruent to the identity modulo $y$, then there exist $\theta\in\operatorname{Aut}(S)$, $c\in W$ and a $2\times 2$ matrix $M$ over $W$ such that $\theta$ intertwines $e\circ\mathrm{toC}\circ\mathrm{germY}$ with the action of $\tau$ on $C$, $\theta$ fixes every constant class $\mathrm{mkS}(C(w))$, $\theta(\mathrm{mkS}\,X_{j})\equiv \mathrm{mkS}\big(\sum_i C(M_{ij})X_i\big)$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by $\mathrm{mkS}\,X_0,\mathrm{mkS}\,X_1$, $c^{q+1}\equiv 1$ and $M_{ij}\equiv c\,\gamma_{ij}$ modulo $\mathfrak m_W$, $\gamma_{11}\equiv 1\pmod{\ell}$ forces $c\equiv 1$ modulo $\mathfrak m_W$, and if $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity on $K$ then $c-1\notin\mathfrak m_W$.
--
--   Under these antecedents the conclusion is the following. Let $d\in(\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, let $\sigma_L$ be a ring automorphism of $L$ and $\sigma_A$ one of $A$ with $\sigma_L$ extending $\sigma_A$ along $A\to L$, with $\sigma_A(a)\equiv a$ modulo $\mathfrak m_A$ for all $a$. Let $\pi\in A$ satisfy $\pi^{q^2-1}=q$, let $\alpha\in A$ satisfy $\sigma_A\pi=\alpha\pi$ and $\alpha^{q+1}\equiv \mathrm{val}(d)$ modulo $\mathfrak m_A$, where $\mathrm{val}(d)$ is the natural-number representative of $d$ mapped into $A$. Let $\tau$ be a ring automorphism of $K$ acting on Laurent series as the coefficientwise map induced by $\sigma_L$. Then:
--
--   (1) $\tau$ maps the finite chart algebra $C$ into itself; and
--
--   (2) for every proof `hpres` of that preservation: (a) the induced endomorphism of $C$ satisfies $\tau(a)-a\in y$ for all $a\in C$; and (b) there exist a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, a ring automorphism $\sigma_W$ of $W$, an element $ct\in W$ and a $2\times2$ matrix $M$ over $W$ such that
--
--   • $\theta\big(e(\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}\,a))\big)=e(\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(\tau a)))$ for all $a\in C$;
--
--   • $\sigma_W\circ\sigma=\sigma\circ\sigma_A$ on $A$;
--
--   • $\sigma_W(w)\equiv w$ modulo $\mathfrak m_W$ for all $w\in W$;
--
--   • $\theta(\mathrm{mkS}(C(w)))=\mathrm{mkS}(C(\sigma_W w))$ for all $w\in W$, so $\theta$ is $\sigma_W$-semilinear on constants;
--
--   • for each $j\in\{0,1\}$, $\theta(\mathrm{mkS}\,X_j)-\mathrm{mkS}\big(\sum_i C(M_{ij})X_i\big)$ lies in the square of the ideal generated by $\mathrm{mkS}\,X_0$ and $\mathrm{mkS}\,X_1$;
--
--   • $ct-\sigma(\alpha^{q+1})\in\mathfrak m_W$;
--
--   • for all $i,j$, $M_{ij}-ct\cdot\big(\text{the }(i,j)\text{ entry of }\mathrm{diagOneElem}\,q\,(d^{q})^{-1}\big)\in\mathfrak m_W$, the entries of $\mathrm{diagOneElem}\,q\,(d^q)^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&(d^{q})^{-1}\end{pmatrix}$ over $\mathbb{Z}/q$ being read into $W$ through their natural-number representatives.
--
--   Thus the linear part of $\theta$ is, modulo $\mathfrak m_W$, the scalar $ct\equiv\sigma(\alpha^{q+1})$ times $\mathrm{diag}(1,d^{-q})$.
--
--   This is the computation of the action of an inertia element at $q$ on the completed local ring at a supersingular point of the integral model of the modular curve of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$, expressed in Drinfeld coordinates: the automorphism is semilinear over an automorphism of the coefficient ring $W$ congruent to the identity modulo $\mathfrak m_W$, and its linear part is a scalar multiple of $\mathrm{diag}(1,d^{-q})$, with the scalar pinned to $\sigma(\alpha^{q+1})$ where $\alpha$ records the action on a root $\pi$ of $X^{q^2-1}=q$. It is used in the assembly of supersingular affine charts with their separating and inertia data for the auxiliary level-one analysis.
